and we're back in our series a more  perfect union we aim to show that what  unites us as Americans is far greater  than what divides us as the corona virus  continues to spread neighbors are  forming mutual aid groups to help each  other out the concept is not new but  it's made a huge difference in places  badly affected by the pandemic places  like New York City Eric Barnett shows us  how one family in Queens created a  massive aid network as helping hundreds  of people avians op Ellis has lived in  Astoria for 22 years  most of her neighbors were strangers  until the pandemic brought them together  in one of the hardest hit regions in the  country people are banding together  volunteering for grocery runs and other  errands to help the most vulnerable I  immediately reached my apartment I need  medication there really yeah that's what  we're here for  it's been a lifeline for people like  Alissa Weinberger who lives with the  autoimmune disease lupus it really  helped me keep my independence and my  sense of normalcy what began with flyers  and texts has evolved into the historia  mutual aid network I said to them you  know I'm a nurse can I help in any way  I'd love to help and they're like  absolutely volunteer Marie eliris became  a neighbor in need after she and her  husband contracted kovat 19 leaving  their 11 year old daughter to care for  them both we were bedridden  we were very weak I was shocked how it  can hit you like a ton of bricks how  much help did you receive from the group  you were previously volunteering I was  thankful that they were out there when I  was first really sick with like  temperatures spiking to 102 103 four  days in a row I remember the one lady  who I had asked her for cleaning wipes  for us but I ended up using them to like  keep cool and I really think that helped  me bring the temperature down  and her husband bounced it back but her  sister Anna who also contracted the  virus couldn't she little older with  some underlying health conditions and  unfortunately despite their best efforts  she passed away this mutual aid network  was an idea born out of this pretty  nondescript building here in Astoria a  woman who lives here realized the  neighbor needed groceries so she took  what she could out of a fridge and  hand-delivered it that good deed has  cascaded into hundreds of neighbors  helping each other we know we've been  able to assist over 600 of our neighbors  they've asked our support for at least  900 tasks and we've been able to do that  because we have an incredible network of  almost 900 now volunteers Miriam and her  husband Ross created the Astoria mutual  aid Network coughing out a deep sense of  community for their seven-year-old  daughter Emma  even though the New York metro area is  densely populated that's often the case  that you don't know who lives right next  door  yeah for me to truly get to know my  community and the members of that  community in a very direct and real way  has been incredibly powerful what do you  think will be the lasting impact of what  you're doing my hope is that we're not  waiting to go back to what we call  normal but that this experiences helps  us shape what we want the new normal to  be leading with compassion and humanity  and recognizing that we have the agency  to create a change and to demand the  change within our communities that we  hope to see CBS this morning  Errol Barnett New York Wow Bravo Errol  and Bravo to the Astoria mutual age this  is one of those moments where you wonder  could this be a longer term change in  the way we help one another instead of  turning to private services turn to your  neighbors turn to your friends they're  there two guys built something special
 

